\begin_layout Standard
Based on the graph from Part C,
 the effect of diminishing returns can be observed immediately after the first and second hidden layers.
 Adding one layer significantly boosts the accuracy from around 0.800 to nearly 0.838.
 The accuracy reaches its peak at 2 hidden layers (approaching 0.840),
 drops slightly at 3 layers,
 and rises again a little at 4 layers.
 These results are completely expected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A DAN model works by computing a simple average of the word embeddings,
 meaning that the input to the network is a simple,
 orderless representation of the text.
 While adding one or two layers helps the network learn non-linear combinations of these averaged features,
 adding too many layers (such as 3 or 4) to such a simple and orderless input often leads to overfitting or instability,
 which explains why the performance stabilizes or decreases slightly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The linear model (0 hidden layers) did not achieve better performance than the 4-hidden-layer model.
 The graph clearly shows that the model with 0 hidden layers had the lowest accuracy in the group (approximately 0.800),
 while the 4-hidden-layer model reached an accuracy of about 0.839.
\end_layout

