\begin_layout Subsection*
5.1.a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I will discuss two intrinsic evaluation metrics:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Coherence-
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
measure how much a certain topic is coherent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Namely,
 how much top words that belong to the topic are likely to be seen near each other in the corpus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 given the following words inside a topic:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surf sea wave shore
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 will give higher coherence then 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surf champion banana car
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
simply because the first words are more likely to appear in the same context.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mathematical expression for the coherence:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First we will choose some top matching words 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 for a certain topic 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Denote 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(w\right)$
\end_inset

 the probability to get the word 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 from some context,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(u,w\right)$
\end_inset

 is the joint distribution ,
 ie to see both words in the same context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{Coherence}\left(T\right) & =\frac{1}{\left|W\right|}\sum_{\left(w,u\right)\in W}\log\left(\frac{P\left(w,u\right)}{P\left(w\right)P\left(u\right)}\right)\\
\text{Coherence} & =\frac{1}{K}\sum^{K}_{i=1}\text{Coherence}\left(T_{i}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Diversity-
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The diversity metric aims to measure how much the topics differ.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We measure how likely that a word will appear in both topics in the following method:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Choose 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{k}$
\end_inset

 set of most likely words for each topic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 topics and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 top words per topic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\textnormal{Diversity}=\frac{\left|\bigcup^{K}_{k=1}W_{k}\right|}{K\cdot N}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

