\begin_layout Subsection*
5.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I used the two coherence parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $c\_v$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\_uci$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both parameters use a sliding window to check if some words appear together,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\_v$
\end_inset

 is more sophisticated with additional statistical methods and different normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I got the following results:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
c\_v & =0.41\\
c\_uci & =-5.97
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first parameter range 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le c\_v\le1$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 means no coherence at all and 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 means very strong coherence.
 We got 
\begin_inset Formula $0.41$
\end_inset

 so we can see a coherence was detected but not that high,
 probably due to real world messy text data or not enough data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $c\_uci\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 however most of times it falls approximately in 
\begin_inset Formula $[-15,5]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It shows a medium coherence measure which probably results from its strict window of length 
\begin_inset Formula $10$
\end_inset

,
 some words appear for example in the next sentence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One problem is semantics,
 namely the literal meaning of words.
 The two evaluation parameters I have shown earlier can fail on this regard.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example a topic may contain top words which are written close to each other many times and therefore produce high coherence,
 but the words are very different semantically.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another example is a model with very low diversity which may seem bad.
 But a human may recognize a lot of topics being merged together in the corpus,
 for example science of music.
 
\end_layout

